<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ionians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ionians were one of the main groups of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, speaking the Ionic dialect and living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> and, from about 1000 BCE, the central west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>. Their twelve cities there, including Miletus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> and Chios, formed the Ionian League. Ionia was the birthplace of Greek philosophy and science.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ionians.jpg" alt="Ionians" width="320" height="552" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.Davey · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xerxes_I_tomb_Ionian_soldier_circa_470_BCE_cleaned_up.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> of the ancient period saw themselves as divided into four major tribes: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolians">Aeolians</a>, Achaeans and Ionians. The term Ionian had several senses. In the narrowest it meant the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. More broadly it covered all speakers of the Ionic dialect, including the Greek populations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> and many cities founded by Ionian colonists, and in the broadest sense all speakers of the East Greek group, which included Attic.</p><p>The foundation myth current in the Classical period held that the Ionians were named after Ion, son of Xuthus, who lived in Aigialeia in the north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorians</a> invaded the Peloponnese they expelled the Achaeans from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolid</a> and Lacedaemonia, and the displaced Achaeans moved into Aigialeia, thereafter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, expelling the Ionians. The Ionians went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, mingled with its people and later emigrated to the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>. Herodotus says that all are Ionians who are of Athenian descent and keep the feast Apaturia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name appears in the records of many civilisations. A fragmentary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> bears the name i-ja-wo-ne, interpreted as possibly an ethnic name, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> uses Ἰάονες once, of long-robed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> apparently identified with Athenians. Letters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> from the 8th century BCE record attacks by what appear to be Ionians on Phoenician cities, including a raid reported to Tiglath-Pileser III in a letter from the 730s BCE. Old Persian inscriptions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> list the Yaunā among the empire's provinces. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a>, Javan, a son of Japheth, is believed by most Bible scholars to represent the Ionians.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edicts-of-ashoka">Edicts of Ashoka</a> refer to the Yonas, in the Thirteenth Edict dated to 260–258 BCE. Ionic Greek, spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> and, from about 1000 BCE, Asiatic Ionia, was the base of the literary language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>, though it was eventually supplanted by Attic, dominant in the Greek world by the 5th century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Unlike the austere and militaristic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorians</a>, the Ionians are known for their love of philosophy, art, democracy and pleasure, traits most clearly expressed by the Athenians. In the 6th century BCE the coastal towns of Miletus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> became the focus of a revolution in thinking about nature. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales</a> and his successors, called physiologoi, were sceptical of religious explanations and sought mechanical and physical ones. The physicist Carlo Rovelli regards the work of the Ionian school as the first great scientific revolution.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ionians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ionian Greeks</b> settled the island around 1000 BCE, and Chios became one of the twelve members of the Ionian League, which met at the Panionion on Mount Mycale.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Above the city stood the Amphictyonic League, or Amphictyony, an ancient association of twelve peoples, mostly of central and northern Greece, among them the Thessalians, Boeotians, Phocians, Locrians, Dorians and <b>Ionians</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the collapse of the Bronze Age world, <b>Ionian Greeks</b> settled Miletus around 1000 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek tradition held that the island had earlier been settled by Carians and Leleges and that Ionian colonists arrived under a leader named Procles from Epidaurus; whatever the details, <b>Ionian Greeks</b> were established on Samos by about 1000 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Twelve peoples, among them the Thessalians, Phocians, Boeotians, Dorians and <b>Ionians</b>, each sent two delegates (hieromnemones) to a council meeting twice a year at Anthela and Delphi.</blockquote><a href="/state/amphictyonic-league">Amphictyonic League</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 6th century BCE Bias of Priene, counted among the Seven Sages, advised the <b>Ionians</b> to emigrate to Sardinia rather than submit to Persia.</blockquote><a href="/state/priene">Priene</a></li></ul></section>
